From bc15dedcc6f779567aa77cc0a4d2843d593474da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ond=C5=99ej=20=C5=A0imon?= <ondrej@dorhawk.software>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 18:39:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 64/65] winemac.drv: Give each display its own pixels per point
 in Retina mode.

Retina mode gave every display two pixels per point, so on a display beside a Retina one (a 1x
external monitor) a program drew twice the pixels the display has and macOS shrank them: blurry
text and lines. Each display now has its own scale: 2 on a Retina display, 1 on others, and its
own DPI (192 and 96). A display's origin in pixels is its origin in points times the largest
scale, so the desktop's coordinates stay whole and in order.

The Cocoa side converts between points and pixels by the display a point or rect is on, a
window's surface, shape and views by the display the window is on, and a window's largest size by
the display with the fewest pixels per point. A window moved to a display of another scale is
placed again with the title bar and borders win32u gives it there, so its rect matches the frame
macOS shows.

A maximized window that a window tool moves to another display (patch 0052) is judged in points:
its size, its gaps to the work area and its top. The frame that fills the new display keeps the
visible rect's distances to the area's edges, scaled to the new display, with the window's own
edges where win32u now puts them; a window that no longer fills a display is restored at once,
then kept where the tool left it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
